{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r4s0.yaml; rg -n 'hypothesis.schema|jsonschema|validate.*hypothesis' tools schemas Makefile pyproject.toml 2>/dev/null | head -80\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260711-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Replace full register-file reset with validity bits\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The register file synchronously clears all 32 data words, placing roughly one\n  thousand state bits on reset and per-word write-enable muxing. On the FPGA this\n  wide reset structure consumes routing and can prevent the array from being\n  implemented as a compact memory, even though RV32 does not require nonzero\n  registers to contain a particular value after reset.\nhypothesis: |\n  Leave the 31 writable register data words unreset and add one resettable valid\n  bit per word. Reads of an invalid word return zero, writes set its valid bit,\n  and the existing same-cycle write-first bypass remains highest priority. This\n  preserves the core's current deterministic zero-after-reset behavior and x0\n  semantics while shrinking the reset network from 1024 data bits to 31 flags.\n  The simpler data array should reduce reset routing and mux pressure, improve\n  memory-style inference or packing, and raise median Fmax without changing any\n  CoreMark instruction cycles or RVFI-visible register values.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Remove reset writes to the register data array, add resettable per-register valid bits, return zero for unwritten registers, and preserve x0 plus same-cycle write-first behavior.\"\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: \"Extend reset, rewrite, and post-reset read tests to prove validity bits reproduce zero-after-reset semantics and do not disturb write-first bypass or x0 hardwiring.\"\ntools/tournament.py:54:# The hypothesis schema's `category` enum, in the order the brief specifies.\ntools/tournament.py:218:    import jsonschema\ntools/tournament.py:220:        emit_verilog, offlimits_changes, _read_notes, validate_hypothesis,\ntools/tournament.py:261:        hyp = validate_hypothesis(hyp_path)\ntools/tournament.py:262:    except (jsonschema.ValidationError, FileNotFoundError, yaml.YAMLError) as e:\ntools/orchestrator.py:6:import jsonschema, yaml\ntools/orchestrator.py:48:HYP_SCHEMA     = json.loads(Path(\"schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\").read_text())\ntools/orchestrator.py:444:def validate_hypothesis(hyp_path: str) -> dict:\ntools/orchestrator.py:447:    jsonschema.validate(hyp, HYP_SCHEMA)\ntools/agents/static_agent.py:94:        \"      No-op placeholder to satisfy the hypothesis schema's\\n\"\ntools/agents/hypothesis.py:282:## Required YAML structure (validates against schemas/hypothesis.schema.json)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
