g:98:Info:   at iteration #10: temp = 0.000000, timing cost = 35, wirelen = 42620\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:99:Info:   at iteration #15: temp = 0.000000, timing cost = 50, wirelen = 41384\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:100:Info:   at iteration #20: temp = 0.000000, timing cost = 62, wirelen = 40118\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:101:Info:   at iteration #25: temp = 0.000000, timing cost = 41, wirelen = 39519\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:102:Info:   at iteration #29: temp = 0.000000, timing cost = 73, wirelen = 39337 \ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:105:Info: Max frequency for clock 'cpu.clock': 136.91 MHz (PASS at 12.00 MHz)\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:107:Info: Slack histogram:\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:257:Info: Critical path report for clock 'cpu.clock' (posedge -> posedge):\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:312:Info:      setup  0.04  7.77 Source cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_72.D\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:315:Info: Max frequency for clock 'cpu.clock': 128.72 MHz (PASS at 12.00 MHz)\ncores/bench/generated/pnr_seed1/nextpnr.log:317:Info: Slack histogram:\n{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"019f534c-df6f-7682-930a-3321a666021a\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll inspect the curated lessons and the micro-opt experiment history, then write and validate the single hypothesis file.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n 'micro_opt|hyp-20260711-001' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 80\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n 'micro_opt|hyp-20260711-001' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 80\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +34.28%): Moving rare CoreMark DIV/REM operations from the single-cycle ALU into a shared radix-2 iterative unit removes costly combinational `/` and `%` networks from the common execute path, yielding a large FPGA timing/placement gain despite multi-cycle divide stalls.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (regression, -12.77%): Even a 16-entry tagged BTB leaves an indexed tag/counter/target read and taken mux on the fetch-PC feedback path, so its timing cost can outweigh CoreMark's saved EX redirect bubbles unless lookup is pipelined or decoupled.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use detection must also zero unused ID/RVFI source addresses, or raw immediate bits remain reported as register reads without the old protective stall and riscv-formal's reg check catches stale source data.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r2s1 (improvement, +8.71%): A readiness-qualified, width/sign-corrected MEM-to-EX load bypass removes CoreMark pointer-chasing load-use bubbles, provided dmem backpressure still freezes the pipeline and suppresses dependent redirects until the response is valid.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -0.20%): Even table-free backward-branch/JAL prediction is slightly net-negative when immediate decode, a 32-bit target adder, and a taken mux remain combinational on the fetch-PC feedback path, so avoiding EX redirect bubbles requires a decoupled or pipelined target path.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r3s0 (regression, -4.03%): Sharing the main ALU adder for branch/JAL/JALR targets can worsen FPGA fitness because control-flow operand selects and the ALU result mux move onto the redirect path, outweighing removal of dedicated target adders and dead EX/MEM metadata.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -23.00%): A registered-only two-entry fetch queue adds an empty refill cycle after every EX redirect, so CoreMark's increased control-flow recovery penalty can overwhelm the limited benefit of overlapping independent imem stalls with dmem/divider backpressure unless the queue has fall-through bypass or prediction.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -18.42%): Even a tag-free four-entry RAS regresses fitness when canonical-RET predecode and a 32-bit ras_top mux remain combinational in the fetch-PC feedback path, so return prediction must be decoupled or staged rather than selected directly from imem_data.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (regression, -16.95%): Replacing register-data reset with per-entry validity bits can worsen FPGA timing because asynchronous reads gain indexed validity checks and zeroing muxes while the combinational 2R1W array still cannot infer a compact memory, so reducing reset fanout alone is not necessarily a win.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -37.23%): Registering a lookahead BTB result does not guarantee timing decoupling when the same cycle still runs fetch PC through a PC+4 adder, tagged table lookup, and direction logic into those registers; that reg-to-reg cone can replace the fetch feedback loop as the Fmax limiter.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r5s1 (regression, -16.30%): Retiming the branch/JAL PC-plus-immediate adder into decode can worsen FPGA fitness because the always-active 32-bit sum and added ID/EX target routing outweigh shortening the EX redirect cone when CoreMark's branch penalty and cycle count are unchanged.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Collapsing MEM/WB cannot retire a load combinationally when EX/MEM first presents its address, because `io_dmemReady` accepts the transaction but the memory model still needs the MEM cycle to produce sampleable `io_dmemRData`; fused retirement therefore needs an explicit load-response-valid boundary.\\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -52.48%): Narrowing a backward-branch target calculation to a same-page 9-bit add does not rescue fetch timing when B-immediate legality/range decode and the predicted-target mux still form an instruction-dependent PC feedback cone, so short-loop prediction must be staged or decoupled rather than merely width-reduced.\\n1:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-2a03fe4\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T20:33:25.843120Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n2:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share one multiplier across all RV32M multiply variants\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The combinational ALU currently describes three independent 64-bit products for\\\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. All three\\\\nexist in the synthesized datapath even though a single-issue instruction can use\\\\nonly one multiply result, consuming scarce LUTs and routing resources on a design\\\\nalready at 9563 LUT4s and likely depressing Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The combinational ALU currently describes three independent 64-bit products for\\\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. All three\\\\nexist in the synthesized datapath even though a single-issue instruction can use\\\\nonly one multiply result, consuming scarce LUTs and routing resources on a design\\\\nalready at 9563 LUT4s and likely depressing Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Compute one 64-bit unsigned product a*b and derive the signed high-half results\\\\nwith the standard modulo-2^32 corrections: MULH subtracts b when a is negative\\\\nand a when b is negative, while MULHSU subtracts b only when a is negative.\\\\nMUL and MULHU select the low and high halves of the shared product directly.\\\\nKeeping the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch unchanged preserves tractable\\\\nformal checks. This removes two full multipliers without adding pipeline stalls,\\\\nso CoreMark cycles should remain constant while reduced area and routing improve\\\\nmedian FPGA Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 product and correction-subtractor logic for MULH and MULHSU, preserving all RV32M edge semantics and the ALTOPS branch.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_alu.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add focused sign-boundary and randomized multiply-high vectors that verify the shared-product correction formulas for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 product and correction-subtractor logic for MULH and MULHSU, preserving all RV32M edge semantics and the ALTOPS branch.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_alu.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add focused sign-boundary and randomized multiply-high vectors that verify the shared-product correction formulas for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 369.9, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 30.79, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 30.79, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 166.14, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 10476, \\\"ff\\\": 1875, \\\"seeds\\\": [170.44, 145.92, 166.14], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T20:42:42.630120Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 1}\\n5:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Qualify load-use hazards by actual source operands\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The load-use detector compares a load destination against both raw rs1 and\\\\nrs2 bitfields of the following instruction. Those fields are not both source\\\\nregisters for many RV32 formats: OP-IMM, loads, and JALR do not read rs2,\\\\nwhile LUI, AUIPC, and JAL read neither field. Coincidental matches therefore\\\\ninject unnecessary bubbles into CoreMark without protecting any dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The load-use detector compares a load destination against both raw rs1 and\\\\nrs2 bitfields of the following instruction. Those fields are not both source\\\\nregisters for many RV32 formats: OP-IMM, loads, and JALR do not read rs2,\\\\nwhile LUI, AUIPC, and JAL read neither field. Coincidental matches therefore\\\\ninject unnecessary bubbles into CoreMark without protecting any dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Derive compact uses-rs1 and uses-rs2 qualifiers from the following\\\\ninstruction's opcode and gate each load-destination comparison with the\\\\ncorresponding qualifier. Preserve the existing one-cycle bubble for every\\\\ntrue dependency, including store data and branch operands, while allowing\\\\nfalse matches in immediate and upper-immediate encodings to proceed. This\\\\nremoves avoidable cycles with only shallow opcode-decode logic off the main\\\\nexecute datapath, so CoreMark iterations per cycle should improve with\\\\nnegligible Fmax or area cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which source operands the IF/ID opcode actually reads and gate the rs1 and rs2 load-use comparisons accordingly, while retaining all existing redirect, divider, and bus-stall priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pass the complete IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit so source-use qualification is based on the pending instruction without changing the nret=1 interface or retirement behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed adjacent-load cases proving true OP, OP-IMM, store-data, and branch dependencies still stall and false matches embedded in unused immediate, LUI, AUIPC, and JAL fields do not create bubbles.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which source operands the IF/ID opcode actually reads and gate the rs1 and rs2 load-use comparisons accordingly, while retaining all existing redirect, divider, and bus-stall priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pass the complete IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit so source-use qualification is based on the pending instruction without changing the nret=1 interface or retirement behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed adjacent-load cases proving true OP, OP-IMM, store-data, and branch dependencies still stall and false matches embedded in unused immediate, LUI, AUIPC, and JAL fields do not create bubbles.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\\\ne.vcd\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:23  Writing trace 