ns/gpt-5_6-sol-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-001-r2s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-24115/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 2, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use detection must also zero unused ID/RVFI source addresses, or raw immediate bits remain reported as register reads without the old protective stall and riscv-formal's reg check catches stale source data.\\\"}\\n8:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Collapse redundant execute-stage target adders\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The execute stage currently builds branch targets with a dedicated PC-plus-immediate\\\\nadder and JALR targets with another rs1-plus-immediate adder, while the main ALU also\\\\nperforms an unused ADD for those same control-flow instructions. These parallel\\\\n32-bit adders and their routing consume LUTs and placement freedom on the redirect\\\\npath, and branch metadata is then clocked into EX/MEM even though no later stage uses it.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The execute stage currently builds branch targets with a dedicated PC-plus-immediate\\\\nadder and JALR targets with another rs1-plus-immediate adder, while the main ALU also\\\\nperforms an unused ADD for those same control-flow instructions. These parallel\\\\n32-bit adders and their routing consume LUTs and placement freedom on the redirect\\\\npath, and branch metadata is then clocked into EX/MEM even though no later stage uses it.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Select PC and the decoded immediate as the main ALU operands for conditional branches\\\\nand direct JAL, and select forwarded rs1 and the immediate for JALR, allowing the ALU's\\\\nexisting ADD result to serve as the sole control-flow target sum. Apply JALR bit-zero\\\\nclearing after that shared result, while leaving the separate comparators, redirect\\\\nrules, misalignment traps, PC-plus-four link value, and dmem-stall suppression intact.\\\\nRemove the dead branch_taken and branch_target fields from EX/MEM so they no longer\\\\nconsume flops or routing. Eliminating redundant adders and payload should improve\\\\nmedian FPGA Fmax with no CoreMark cycle penalty and no change to architectural state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Route branch, JAL, and JALR target arithmetic through the existing ALU ADD path; preserve forwarding, JALR alignment, redirect gating, traps, link writes, divider interlocks, and RVFI pc_wdata behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target members from ex_mem_t to reduce EX/MEM register width and downstream routing.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update the EX/MEM payload usage annotations after pruning dead branch metadata, without changing memory or RVFI behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed taken and not-taken branches plus JAL and forwarded JALR cases, including misaligned targets and bus stalls, to verify shared target arithmetic and exact RVFI next PCs.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Route branch, JAL, and JALR target arithmetic through the existing ALU ADD path; preserve forwarding, JALR alignment, redirect gating, traps, link writes, divider interlocks, and RVFI pc_wdata behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target members from ex_mem_t to reduce EX/MEM register width and downstream routing.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update the EX/MEM payload usage annotations after pruning dead branch metadata, without changing memory or RVFI behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed taken and not-taken branches plus JAL and forwarded JALR cases, including misaligned targets and bus stalls, to verify shared target arithmetic and exact RVFI next PCs.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 396.24, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -4.03, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 40.1, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 169.89, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4287593, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 5620, \\\"ff\\\": 2200, \\\"seeds\\\": [166.58, 169.89, 173.25], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T21:22:21.346402Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 3, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r3s0 (regression, -4.03%): Sharing the main ALU adder for branch/JAL/JALR targets can worsen FPGA fitness because control-flow operand selects and the ALU result mux move onto the redirect path, outweighing removal of dedicated target adders and dead EX/MEM metadata.\\\"}\\n11:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replace full register-file reset with validity bits\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The register file synchronously clears all 32 data words, placing roughly one\\\\nthousand state bits on reset and per-word write-enable muxing. On the FPGA this\\\\nwide reset structure consumes routing and can prevent the array from being\\\\nimplemented as a compact memory, even though RV32 does not require nonzero\\\\nregisters to contain a particular value after reset.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The register file synchronously clears all 32 data words, placing roughly one\\\\nthousand state bits on reset and per-word write-enable muxing. On the FPGA this\\\\nwide reset structure consumes routing and can prevent the array from being\\\\nimplemented as a compact memory, even though RV32 does not require nonzero\\\\nregisters to contain a particular value after reset.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Leave the 31 writable register data words unreset and add one resettable valid\\\\nbit per word. Reads of an invalid word return zero, writes set its valid bit,\\\\nand the existing same-cycle write-first bypass remains highest priority. This\\\\npreserves the core's current deterministic zero-after-reset behavior and x0\\\\nsemantics while shrinking the reset network from 1024 data bits to 31 flags.\\\\nThe simpler data array should reduce reset routing and mux pressure, improve\\\\nmemory-style inference or packing, and raise median Fmax without changing any\\\\nCoreMark instruction cycles or RVFI-visible register values.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove reset writes to the register data array, add resettable per-register valid bits, return zero for unwritten registers, and preserve x0 plus same-cycle write-first behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_reg_file.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend reset, rewrite, and post-reset read tests to prove validity bits reproduce zero-after-reset semantics and do not disturb write-first bypass or x0 hardwiring.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove reset writes to the register data array, add resettable per-register valid bits, return zero for unwritten registers, and preserve x0 plus same-cycle write-first behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_reg_file.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend reset, rewrite, and post-reset read tests to prove validity bits reproduce zero-after-reset semantics and do not disturb write-first bypass or x0 hardwiring.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 342.9, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -16.95, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 21.24, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 147.02, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4287593, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 3353, \\\"ff\\\": 1428, \\\"seeds\\\": [147.02, 166.61, 141.14], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T21:37:15.130750Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 4, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (regression, -16.95%): Replacing register-data reset with per-entry validity bits can worsen FPGA timing because asynchronous reads gain indexed validity checks and zeroing muxes while the combinational 2R1W array still cannot infer a compact memory, so reducing reset fanout alone is not necessarily a win.\\\"}\\n14:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replace load barrel shift with lane muxes\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The successful MEM-to-EX load bypass made load extraction part of a long\\\\nsame-cycle path from io_dmemRData through forwarding and the consumer ALU or\\\\nbranch comparator into EX/MEM. The current generic 32-bit variable right\\\\nshift moves far more data than an RV32 byte or halfword load needs and may\\\\nconsume both LUT depth and routing on this now performance-critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The successful MEM-to-EX load bypass made load extraction part of a long\\\\nsame-cycle path from io_dmemRData through forwarding and the consumer ALU or\\\\nbranch comparator into EX/MEM. The current generic 32-bit variable right\\\\nshift moves far more data than an RV32 byte or halfword load needs and may\\\\nconsume both LUT depth and routing on this now performance-critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the generic dmem_rdata shift with width-specific direct lane selects:\\\\na four-way 8-bit mux for byte loads, a two-way 16-bit mux for aligned\\\\nhalfword loads, and an unmodified word path. Perform sign or zero extension\\\\nonly after the narrow mux, and express store masks with explicit address\\\\ncases rather than variable shifts. This preserves every load value, memory\\\\nmask, misalignment trap, bypass qualification, and pipeline cycle while\\\\nreducing the combinational depth and switched routing between memory and EX,\\\\nso median Fmax should improve without an IPC penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the generic 32-bit load shift and variable store-mask shift with narrow explicit byte/halfword lane muxes and address-case masks, preserving alignment traps, RVFI fields, and the same-cycle load bypass.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed LB/LBU cases at every byte offset and LH/LHU cases at both aligned halfword offsets, including adjacent dependent consumers and dmem stalls, to verify lane selection, extension, bypass timing, and RVFI masks.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the generic 32-bit load shift and variable store-mask shift with narrow explicit byte/halfword lane muxes and address-case masks, preserving alignment traps, RVFI fields, and the same-cycle load bypass.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed LB/LBU cases at every byte offset and LH/LHU cases at both aligned halfword offsets, including adjacent dependent consumers and dmem stalls, to verify lane selection, extension, bypass timing, and RVFI masks.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 381.22, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -7.67, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 34.79, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 163.45, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4287593, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 5730, \\\"ff\\\": 2200, \\\"seeds\\\": [163.45, 155.28, 166.22], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T22:07:50.951443Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 5}\\n17:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Narrow post-execute pipeline metadata\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The full decode control bundle is clocked through both EX/MEM and MEM/WB even\\\\nthough most of its fields become dead after execute. EX/MEM also retains two\\\\nbranch-result fields that no downstream stage reads. These unnecessary flops\\\\nand high-fanout routes consume placement freedom around the already dense\\\\nexecute and memory stages without contributing to CoreMark throughput.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The full decode control bundle is clocked through both EX/MEM and MEM/WB even\\\\nthough most of its fields become dead after execute. EX/MEM also retains two\\\\nbranch-result fields that no downstream stage reads. These unnecessary flops\\\\nand high-fanout routes consume placement freedom around the already dense\\\\nexecute and memory stages without contributing to CoreMark throughput.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Introduce stage-specific post-EX and post-MEM control payloads and retain only\\\\nthe bits consumed downstream: memory access controls in EX/MEM, and writeback\\\\nplus trap controls in MEM/WB. Remove the unused registered branch-taken and\\\\nbranch-target metadata while leaving the existing dedicated target adders and\\\\nredirect datapath unchanged. Unlike the earlier experiment that combined\\\\nmetadata pruning with a slower shared control-flow adder, this isolates the\\\\nregister and routing reduction. CoreMark cycles should remain identical while\\\\nthe smaller pipeline registers and reduced control fanout modestly improve\\\\nplacement and median Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Define narrow EX/MEM and MEM/WB control payloads, use them in the corresponding bundles, and remove downstream-unused branch_taken and branch_target fields.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM for normal and divider-completion paths while preserving all arithmetic, redirect, alignment, stall, and RVFI behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM controls and emit only writeback and trap controls into MEM/WB, preserving memory masks, load bypass, misalignment traps, and held-WB semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update hazard and forwarding control references for the narrowed bundles without changing the nret=1 interface, bus handshakes, or retirement ordering.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/wb_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use the narrowed MEM/WB writeback controls with unchanged register-file write qualification and result selection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed ALU, load/store, illegal-instruction, branch/jump, divider, dependency, and randomized bus-stall coverage to prove metadata pruning is cycle- and RVFI-transparent.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Define narrow EX/MEM and MEM/WB control payloads, use them in the corresponding bundles, and remove downstream-unused branch_taken and branch_target fields.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM for normal and divider-completion paths while preserving all arithmetic, redirect, alignment, stall, and RVFI behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM controls and emit only writeback and trap controls into MEM/WB, preserving memory masks, load bypass, misalignment traps, and held-WB semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Update hazard and forwarding control references for the narrowed bundles without changing the nret=1 interface, bus handshakes, or retirement ordering.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/wb_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use the narrowed MEM/WB writeback controls with unchanged register-file write qualification and result selection.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed ALU, load/store, illegal-instruction, branch/jump, divider, dependency, and randomized bus-stall coverage to prove metadata pruning is cycle- and RVFI-transparent.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 366.66, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -11.19, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 29.64, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 157.21, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4287593, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 5721, \\\"ff\\\": 2200, \\\"seeds\\\": [152.04, 157.21, 160.67], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T22:23:16.139605Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 6}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The strongest untouched micro-opt appears to be reducing divider latency without perturbing the common ALU/fetch paths. I’m checking whether prior logs already explored divider early-outs or higher radix before committing to that angle.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n -i 'divider|divide|div/rem|radix|early.out|power.of.two|quotient' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg --files cores/bench | rg 'coremark|\\\\\\\\.elf\\\"'$|'\\\"\\\\\\\\.bin\\\"'$|'\\\"\\\\\\\\.dis|objdump|test_alu|test_pipeline' | head -n 100\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n -i 'divider|divide|div/rem|radix|early.out|power.of.two|quotient' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg --files cores/bench | rg 'coremark|\\\\\\\\.elf\\\"'$|'\\\"\\\\\\\\.bin\\\"'$|'\\\"\\\\\\\\.dis|objdump|test_alu|test_pipeline' | head -n 100\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:1:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +34.28%): Moving rare CoreMark DIV/REM operations from the single-cycle ALU into a shared radix-2 iterative unit removes costly combinational `/` and `%` networks from the common execute path, yielding a large FPGA timing/placement gain despite multi-cycle divide stalls.\\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:7:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -23.00%): A registered-only two-entry fetch queue adds an empty refill cycle after every EX redirect, so CoreMark's increased control-flow recovery penalty can overwhelm the limited benefit of overlapping independent imem stalls with dmem/divider backpressure unless the queue has fall-through bypass or prediction.\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:3:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move DIV and REM into a cold iterative execution unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current single-cycle ALU places four 32-bit division and remainder\\\\nexpressions in the execute datapath. On an FPGA these operators expand into\\\\nlarge combinational networks that consume scarce LUTs and routing and burden\\\\nFmax on every cycle, even though divide-class instructions are rare in the\\\\ntimed CoreMark workload. The core is already close to its LUT4 target, so\\\\nremoving this cold logic from the one-cycle path should improve both timing\\\\nand placement freedom at little workload-cycle cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current single-cycle ALU places four 32-bit division and remainder\\\\nexpressions in the execute datapath. On an FPGA these operators expand into\\\\nlarge combinational networks that consume scarce LUTs and routing and burden\\\\nFmax on every cycle, even though divide-class instructions are rare in the\\\\ntimed CoreMark workload. The core is already close to its LUT4 target, so\\\\nremoving this cold logic from the one-cycle path should improve both timing\\\\nand placement freedom at little workload-cycle cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU with one radix-2 iterative\\\\ndivider shared by all four operations. A divide-class instruction captures\\\\nits forwarded operands and RVFI payload in EX, holds the younger pipeline,\\\\nemits EX/MEM bubbles while the divider is busy, and advances exactly once\\\\nwhen the result is ready. All ordinary ALU operations remain single-cycle.\\\\nHandle divide-by-zero and signed overflow with the exact RV32M results, and\\\\nretain a short RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS implementation so the normal formal gate\\\\nremains tractable. Because CoreMark seldom divides, the roughly 32-cycle\\\\nlatency is expected to have negligible IPC cost while eliminating the\\\\ncombinational divider from the critical path raises median FPGA Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 15, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/div_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a shared iterative radix-2 divider/remainder FSM with signed operand normalization, quotient/remainder sign restoration, RV32M divide-by-zero and overflow handling, and a bounded-latency ALTOPS branch.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove real combinational division and remainder operators from the one-cycle ALU datapath; preserve the existing one-cycle integer and multiply operations and keep formal opcode behavior consistent with the new divider path.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect divide-class instructions, launch the divider once with post-forwarding operands, retain the instruction and RVFI metadata until completion, inject EX/MEM bubbles while busy, and emit the completed result exactly once without disturbing redirects or dmem stalls.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an EX-long-operation interlock that holds IF and ID while DIV/REM occupies EX, with explicit priority relative to redirects, load-use hazards, and memory backpressure so no instruction is lost or replayed.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the divider busy interlock between EX and the hazard unit while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO, single retirement channel, and strict RVFI order behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_alu.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Retarget DIV/REM correctness checks to the iterative handshake and cover signed/unsigned normal cases, divide by zero, signed overflow, back-to-back operations, bounded completion, and result stability.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add pipeline-level DIV/REM dependency and interlock tests that verify one retirement per operation, correct forwarding of the completed result, and progress across randomized imem/dmem stalls.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 15, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/div_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a shared iterative radix-2 divider/remainder FSM with signed operand normalization, quotient/remainder sign restoration, RV32M divide-by-zero and overflow handling, and a bounded-latency ALTOPS branch.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove real combinational division and remainder operators from the one-cycle ALU datapath; preserve the existing one-cycle integer and multiply operations and keep formal opcode behavior consistent with the new divider path.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect divide-class instructions, launch the divider once with post-forwarding operands, retain the instruction and RVFI metadata until completion, inject EX/MEM bubbles while busy, and emit the completed result exactly once without disturbing redirects or dmem stalls.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an EX-long-operation interlock that holds IF and ID while DIV/REM occupies EX, with explicit priority relative to redirects, load-use hazards, and memory backpressure so no instruction is lost or replayed.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the divider busy interlock between EX and the hazard unit while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO, single retirement channel, and strict RVFI order behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_alu.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Retarget DIV/REM correctness checks to the iterative handshake and cover signed/unsigned normal cases, divide by zero, signed overflow, back-to-back operations, bounded completion, and result stability.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add pipeline-level DIV/REM dependency and interlock tests that verify one retirement per operation, correct forwarding of the completed result, and progress across randomized imem/dmem stalls.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 379.78, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 34.28, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 34.28, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 170.59, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491817, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 5614, \\\"ff\\\": 2195, \\\"seeds\\\": [170.59, 173.7, 166.94], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T20:46:19.815788Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 1, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +34.28%): Moving rare CoreMark DIV/REM operations from the single-cycle ALU into a shared radix-2 iterative unit removes costly combinational `/` and `%` networks from the common execute path, yielding a large FPGA timing/placement gain despite multi-cycle divide stalls.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:5:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Qualify load-use hazards by actual source operands\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The load-use detector compares a load destination against both raw rs1 and\\\\nrs2 bitfields of the following instruction. Those fields are not both source\\\\nregisters for many RV32 formats: OP-IMM, loads, and JALR do not read rs2,\\\\nwhile LUI, AUIPC, and JAL read neither field. Coincidental matches therefore\\\\ninject unnecessary bubbles into CoreMark without protecting any dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The load-use detector compares a load destination against both raw rs1 and\\\\nrs2 bitfields of the following instruction. Those fields are not both source\\\\nregisters for many RV32 formats: OP-IMM, loads, and JALR do not read rs2,\\\\nwhile LUI, AUIPC, and JAL read neither field. Coincidental matches therefore\\\\ninject unnecessary bubbles into CoreMark without protecting any dependency.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Derive compact uses-rs1 and uses-rs2 qualifiers from the following\\\\ninstruction's opcode and gate each load-destination comparison with the\\\\ncorresponding qualifier. Preserve the existing one-cycle bubble for every\\\\ntrue dependency, including store data and branch operands, while allowing\\\\nfalse matches in immediate and upper-immediate encodings to proceed. This\\\\nremoves avoidable cycles with only shallow opcode-decode logic off the main\\\\nexecute datapath, so CoreMark iterations per cycle should improve with\\\\nnegligible Fmax or area cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which source operands the IF/ID opcode actually reads and gate the rs1 and rs2 load-use comparisons accordingly, while retaining all existing redirect, divider, and bus-stall priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pass the complete IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit so source-use qualification is based on the pending instruction without changing the nret=1 interface or retirement behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed adjacent-load cases proving true OP, OP-IMM, store-data, and branch dependencies still stall and false matches embedded in unused immediate, LUI, AUIPC, and JAL fields do not create bubbles.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 1, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which source operands the IF/ID opcode actually reads and gate the rs1 and rs2 load-use comparisons accordingly, while retaining all existing redirect, divider, and bus-stall priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pass the complete IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit so source-use qualification is based on the pending instruction without changing the nret=1 interface or retirement behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed adjacent-load cases proving true OP, OP-IMM, store-data, and branch dependencies still stall and false matches embedded in unused immediate, LUI, AUIPC, and JAL fields do not create bubbles.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\\\ne.vcd\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:23  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:23  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:23  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:23  Status: failed\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:24 (24)\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:14 (14)\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\\\nSBY 18:05:27 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\\\n\\\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\\\nFailed: reg_ch0\\\\n\\\\n--- reg_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\\\nSBY 18:05:03 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\\\nSBY 18:05:03 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\\\nSBY 18:05:04 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:0