{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|category: structural|hyp-20260711-00[123]' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +34.28%): Moving rare CoreMark DIV/REM operations from the single-cycle ALU into a shared radix-2 iterative unit removes costly combinational `/` and `%` networks from the common execute path, yielding a large FPGA timing/placement gain despite multi-cycle divide stalls.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (regression, -12.77%): Even a 16-entry tagged BTB leaves an indexed tag/counter/target read and taken mux on the fetch-PC feedback path, so its timing cost can outweigh CoreMark's saved EX redirect bubbles unless lookup is pipelined or decoupled.\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share one multiplier across all RV32M multiply variants\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently describes three independent 64-bit products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. All three\\nexist in the synthesized datapath even though a single-issue instruction can use\\nonly one multiply result, consuming scarce LUTs and routing resources on a design\\nalready at 9563 LUT4s and likely depressing Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently describes three independent 64-bit products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. All three\\nexist in the synthesized datapath even though a single-issue instruction can use\\nonly one multiply result, consuming scarce LUTs and routing resources on a design\\nalready at 9563 LUT4s and likely depressing Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one 64-bit unsigned product a*b and derive the signed high-half results\\nwith the standard modulo-2^32 corrections: MULH subtracts b when a is negative\\nand a when b is negative, while MULHSU subtracts b only when a is negative.\\nMUL and MULHU select the low and high halves of the shared product directly.\\nKeeping the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch unchanged preserves tractable\\nformal checks. This removes two full multipliers without adding pipeline stalls,\\nso CoreMark cycles should remain constant while reduced area and routing improve\\nmedian FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 product and correction-subtractor logic for MULH and MULHSU, preserving all RV32M edge semantics and the ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused sign-boundary and randomized multiply-high vectors that verify the shared-product correction formulas for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 product and correction-subtractor logic for MULH and MULHSU, preserving all RV32M edge semantics and the ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add focused sign-boundary and randomized multiply-high vectors that verify the shared-product correction formulas for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 369.9, \"delta_pct\": 30.79, \"vs_baseline\": 30.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 166.14, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10476, \"ff\": 1875, \"seeds\": [170.44, 145.92, 166.14], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:42:42.630120Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1}\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM into a cold iterative execution unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current single-cycle ALU places four 32-bit division and remainder\\nexpressions in the execute datapath. On an FPGA these operators expand into\\nlarge combinational networks that consume scarce LUTs and routing and burden\\nFmax on every cycle, even though divide-class instructions are rare in the\\ntimed CoreMark workload. The core is already close to its LUT4 target, so\\nremoving this cold logic from the one-cycle path should improve both timing\\nand placement freedom at little workload-cycle cost.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current single-cycle ALU places four 32-bit division and remainder\\nexpressions in the execute datapath. On an FPGA these operators expand into\\nlarge combinational networks that consume scarce LUTs and routing and burden\\nFmax on every cycle, even though divide-class instructions are rare in the\\ntimed CoreMark workload. The core is already close to its LUT4 target, so\\nremoving this cold logic from the one-cycle path should improve both timing\\nand placement freedom at little workload-cycle cost.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU with one radix-2 iterative\\ndivider shared by all four operations. A divide-class instruction captures\\nits forwarded operands and RVFI payload in EX, holds the younger pipeline,\\nemits EX/MEM bubbles while the divider is busy, and advances exactly once\\nwhen the result is ready. All ordinary ALU operations remain single-cycle.\\nHandle divide-by-zero and signed overflow with the exact RV32M results, and\\nretain a short RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS implementation so the normal formal gate\\nremains tractable. Because CoreMark seldom divides, the roughly 32-cycle\\nlatency is expected to have negligible IPC cost while eliminating the\\ncombinational divider from the critical path raises median FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative radix-2 divider/remainder FSM with signed operand normalization, quotient/remainder sign restoration, RV32M divide-by-zero and overflow handling, and a bounded-latency ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational division and remainder operators from the one-cycle ALU datapath; preserve the existing one-cycle integer and multiply operations and keep formal opcode behavior consistent with the new divider path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect divide-class instructions, launch the divider once with post-forwarding operands, retain the instruction and RVFI metadata until completion, inject EX/MEM bubbles while busy, and emit the completed result exactly once without disturbing redirects or dmem stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX-long-operation interlock that holds IF and ID while DIV/REM occupies EX, with explicit priority relative to redirects, load-use hazards, and memory backpressure so no instruction is lost or replayed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider busy interlock between EX and the hazard unit while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO, single retirement channel, and strict RVFI order behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retarget DIV/REM correctness checks to the iterative handshake and cover signed/unsigned normal cases, divide by zero, signed overflow, back-to-back operations, bounded completion, and result stability.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add pipeline-level DIV/REM dependency and interlock tests that verify one retirement per operation, correct forwarding of the completed result, and progress across randomized imem/dmem stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative radix-2 divider/remainder FSM with signed operand normalization, quotient/remainder sign restoration, RV32M divide-by-zero and overflow handling, and a bounded-latency ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational division and remainder operators from the one-cycle ALU datapath; preserve the existing one-cycle integer and multiply operations and keep formal opcode behavior consistent with the new divider path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect divide-class instructions, launch the divider once with post-forwarding operands, retain the instruction and RVFI metadata until completion, inject EX/MEM bubbles while busy, and emit the completed result exactly once without disturbing redirects or dmem stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX-long-operation interlock that holds IF and ID while DIV/REM occupies EX, with explicit priority relative to redirects, load-use hazards, and memory backpressure so no instruction is lost or replayed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider busy interlock between EX and the hazard unit while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO, single retirement channel, and strict RVFI order behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retarget DIV/REM correctness checks to the iterative handshake and cover signed/unsigned normal cases, divide by zero, signed overflow, back-to-back operations, bounded completion, and result stability.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add pipeline-level DIV/REM dependency and interlock tests that verify one retirement per operation, correct forwarding of the completed result, and progress across randomized imem/dmem stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 379.78, \"delta_pct\": 34.28, \"vs_baseline\": 34.28, \"fmax_mhz\": 170.59, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5614, \"ff\": 2195, \"seeds\": [170.59, 173.7, 166.94], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:46:19.815788Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +34.28%): Moving rare CoreMark DIV/REM operations from the single-cycle ALU into a shared radix-2 iterative unit removes costly combinational `/` and `%` networks from the common execute path, yielding a large FPGA timing/placement gain despite multi-cycle divide stalls.\"}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny tagged BTB with direction counters\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every taken branch or jump is currently discovered in EX, after two younger\\ninstructions have entered the front end and must be discarded. CoreMark has\\nfrequent loop backedges and control-flow transfers, so this fixed redirect\\npenalty directly reduces iterations per cycle. A small BTB supplies an\\nalready-computed target and therefore keeps target generation out of the\\nfetch critical path, which is important because fitness also scales with\\nFPGA Fmax and the design is close to its LUT target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every taken branch or jump is currently discovered in EX, after two younger\\ninstructions have entered the front end and must be discarded. CoreMark has\\nfrequent loop backedges and control-flow transfers, so this fixed redirect\\npenalty directly reduces iterations per cycle. A small BTB supplies an\\nalready-computed target and therefore keeps target generation out of the\\nfetch critical path, which is important because fitness also scales with\\nFPGA Fmax and the design is close to its LUT target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC,\\nwith a 2-bit saturating direction counter per entry. Predict tagged JAL and\\nJALR entries taken unconditionally and conditional branches from the counter;\\nupdate target, type, and counter only when a valid control-flow instruction\\nactually advances through EX. Carry the predicted next PC with the\\ninstruction and recover in EX whenever resolved next PC differs, covering\\nboth taken misses and false-taken predictions. This should remove most loop\\nbackedge penalties and learn stable jump targets while keeping predictor\\nstate and combinational lookup small enough to preserve Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry tagged BTB, control-flow type bits, 2-bit counters, lookup, and accepted-EX update logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC metadata and prediction validity through the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a valid BTB prediction as the fetch next PC and emit the prediction metadata with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata into ID/EX without changing decoder legality behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the prediction against the resolved architectural next PC, recover on either prediction direction error, and expose predictor training information only when EX advances.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor, prediction metadata, recovery path, and stall-qualified training interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat any predictor recovery as a redirect that flushes younger wrong-path work while preserving existing stall priorities.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 16-entry tagged BTB, control-flow type bits, 2-bit counters, lookup, and accepted-EX update logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC metadata and prediction validity through the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a valid BTB prediction as the fetch next PC and emit the prediction metadata with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata into ID/EX without changing decoder legality behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the prediction against the resolved architectural next PC, recover on either prediction direction error, and expose predictor training information only when EX advances.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor, prediction metadata, recovery path, and stall-qualified training interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat any predictor recovery as a redirect that flushes younger wrong-path work while preserving existing stall priorities.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 246.71, \"delta_pct\": -12.77, \"vs_baseline\": -12.77, \"fmax_mhz\": 104.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4234052, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12368, \"ff\": 2922, \"seeds\": [103.31, 104.46, 104.65], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:59:19.742035Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (regression, -12.77%): Even a 16-entry tagged BTB leaves an indexed tag/counter/target read and taken mux on the fetch-PC feedback path, so its timing cost can outweigh CoreMark's saved EX redirect bubbles unless lookup is pipelined or decoupled.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
