{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\"),(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\tTiny tagged BTB with direction counters\tregression\t-12.77\tAdd a 16-entry direct-mapped tagged branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC,\\nwith a 2-bit saturating direction counter per entry. Predict tagged JAL and\\nJALR entries taken unconditionally and conditional branches from the counter;\\nupdate target, type, and counter only when a valid control-flow instruction\\nactually advances through EX. Carry the predicted next PC with the\\ninstruction and recover in EX whenever resolved next PC differs, covering\\nboth taken misses and false-taken predictions. This should remove most loop\\nbackedge penalties and learn stable jump targets while keeping predictor\\nstate and combinational lookup small enough to preserve Fmax.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r2s2\tTable-free backward-branch and JAL prediction\tregression\t-0.2\tAdd a table-free static predictor in fetch that predicts conditional branches\\ntaken only when their B-immediate is negative, predicts direct JAL instructions\\ntaken unconditionally, and predicts all other instructions sequentially.\\nForm the predicted target directly from the fetch PC and instruction immediate,\\nqualify prediction on io_imemReady, and carry only the predicted next PC through\\nID into EX. EX compares that value with the resolved architectural next PC and\\nredirects on either a false-taken or missed-taken prediction; JALR remains\\nresolved in EX. With no entries, tags, counters, target array, or update path,\\nthis should recover much of the observed loop/call cycle reduction while adding\\nonly immediate wiring, one target adder, and a small opcode/direction mux to the\\nfetch path, greatly limiting the Fmax and LUT penalty that defeated the BTB.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r3s2\tTiny return-address stack for canonical returns\tregression\t-18.42\tAdd a four-entry return-address stack that pushes PC+4 when a legal call writing\\nx1 advances through EX and pops when a canonical RET (JALR x0,x1,0) advances.\\nIn fetch, recognize only that canonical RET and, when the stack is nonempty and\\nimem is ready, select the registered stack top as predicted next PC. Carry the\\npredicted next PC with the instruction and let EX redirect only if the resolved\\narchitectural next PC differs, so stale, underflowed, or unusual JALR cases recover\\nnormally. This targets frequent function returns while keeping the added feedback\\npath to a narrow instruction predecode and mux, avoiding the target adder and the\\nindexed tag/counter arrays that caused both earlier predictor regressions.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r4s2\tLookahead registered branch target predictor\tregression\t-37.23\tAdd a small direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit direction counters, but\\nlook it up using the next sequential fetch address rather than the currently\\nfetched address. When an instruction fetch is accepted, latch the hit,\\npredicted target, and predicted-taken result for PC+4; on the following\\ncycle those registered signals are already aligned with that PC and select\\nits next address without tag comparison, instruction decoding, target\\naddition, or array data on the PC feedback path. Carry the prediction with\\nthe instruction and let EX recover whenever the resolved architectural next\\nPC differs. Invalidate the lookahead result across redirects and nonaccepted\\nfetches, and train only from valid control-flow instructions that actually\\nadvance through EX. This should retain most of the BTB's control-flow cycle\\nsavings while avoiding the combinational fetch loop that caused every prior\\npredictor regression.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r5s2\tJAL-only exact static predictor\tregression\t-8.52\tPredict only aligned JAL instructions in fetch. Recognize the JAL opcode,\\nconstruct its J immediate, and select PC plus that immediate as the next fetch\\naddress when the instruction response is accepted. Carry a single\\njal_predicted bit with the instruction rather than a 32-bit predicted target;\\nEX recomputes the architectural target as it already does and suppresses its\\nredirect only for a valid, aligned JAL carrying that bit. All conditional\\nbranches and JALR remain predict-not-taken and use the unchanged EX recovery\\npath. This preserves the direct-call and unconditional-jump cycle savings of\\nthe nearly neutral table-free experiment while removing its B-immediate\\ndecode, backward-branch policy, false-taken recovery comparisons, and wide\\nprediction metadata, giving the reduced fetch logic a better chance to retain\\nthe current 177 MHz median Fmax.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r6s2\tSame-page short-loop branch predictor\tregression\t-52.48\tPredict only conditional backward branches whose displacement is in the short\\nrange -256 through -2 and whose target remains in the current 256-byte PC page.\\nForm the target with a 9-bit low-PC plus signed-displacement addition; the ninth\\nsum bit both proves that the negative displacement did not borrow across the\\npage and qualifies the prediction, while the upper 24 PC bits pass through\\nunchanged. Carry only a one-bit predicted-taken flag into EX. A correctly taken\\nprediction suppresses the normal redirect, while a not-taken outcome redirects\\nto PC+4; every unqualified branch retains the existing predict-not-taken path.\\nThis captures compact hot loops without predictor state, tags, wide metadata,\\nor a full-width fetch target adder, offering the cycle benefit of the closest\\nprior predictor with a much smaller Fmax penalty.\\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
