EM whenever io_dmemReady is high. CoreMark has\\nmany pointer-chasing and structure-access sequences, so these unconditional\\nbubbles reduce iterations per cycle. Random dmem backpressure must still freeze\\nthe pipeline, but it need not impose an additional bubble after the response.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a readiness-qualified MEM-to-EX load-result bypass and allow a dependent\\ninstruction to enter EX in the same cycle that the older load completes in\\nMEM. Extend forwarding priority so a matching EX/MEM load selects the fully\\nextracted and sign-extended memory result rather than the load address. Remove\\nthe load-use bubble when io_dmemReady is high; when it is low, retain the\\nexisting whole-pipeline freeze until the response arrives, then advance the\\nload and its dependent together exactly once. This structural bypass should\\neliminate a common one-cycle dependency penalty while preserving the nret=1\\nretirement boundary, strict RVFI ordering, memory masks, and all trap behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current load's width-correct, sign- or zero-extended response as a combinational bypass value while retaining the existing MEM/WB and RVFI capture behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish an EX/MEM load producer from an ALU producer and give its completed memory result highest-priority forwarding to matching EX source operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the MEM-load forwarding source to both operand muxes so a dependent ALU, branch, store-data, or address calculation consumes the returned load value directly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with readiness-aware control: freeze on an outstanding dmem response, but do not bubble a dependency in the cycle the load completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire load-response data and load-producer metadata through the forwarding and hazard paths without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or retirement logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add adjacent load-use cases for ALU operands, branches, effective addresses, and store data under zero-wait and randomized dmem stalls, checking results, single retirement, and RVFI memory fields.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current load's width-correct, sign- or zero-extended response as a combinational bypass value while retaining the existing MEM/WB and RVFI capture behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish an EX/MEM load producer from an ALU producer and give its completed memory result highest-priority forwarding to matching EX source operands.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the MEM-load forwarding source to both operand muxes so a dependent ALU, branch, store-data, or address calculation consumes the returned load value directly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with readiness-aware control: freeze on an outstanding dmem response, but do not bubble a dependency in the cycle the load completes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire load-response data and load-producer metadata through the forwarding and hazard paths without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or retirement logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add adjacent load-use cases for ALU operands, branches, effective addresses, and store data under zero-wait and randomized dmem stalls, checking results, single retirement, and RVFI memory fields.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 412.87, \"delta_pct\": 8.71, \"vs_baseline\": 45.98, \"fmax_mhz\": 177.02, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4287593, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5685, \"ff\": 2194, \"seeds\": [177.02, 178.44, 161.29], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:13:05.506526Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r2s1 (improvement, +8.71%): A readiness-qualified, width/sign-corrected MEM-to-EX load bypass removes CoreMark pointer-chasing load-use bubbles, provided dmem backpressure still freezes the pipeline and suppresses dependent redirects until the response is valid.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Table-free backward-branch and JAL prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior tagged BTB reduced the CoreMark cycle count, showing that avoiding\\nEX-stage redirect bubbles has useful IPC value, but its indexed tag, counter,\\ntarget lookup, and fetch-PC mux reduced median Fmax enough to dominate the\\ngain. CoreMark loop backedges and direct calls can be predicted without any\\nlearned state or target storage because their direction and target immediate\\nare already present in the fetched instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The prior tagged BTB reduced the CoreMark cycle count, showing that avoiding\\nEX-stage redirect bubbles has useful IPC value, but its indexed tag, counter,\\ntarget lookup, and fetch-PC mux reduced median Fmax enough to dominate the\\ngain. CoreMark loop backedges and direct calls can be predicted without any\\nlearned state or target storage because their direction and target immediate\\nare already present in the fetched instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a table-free static predictor in fetch that predicts conditional branches\\ntaken only when their B-immediate is negative, predicts direct JAL instructions\\ntaken unconditionally, and predicts all other instructions sequentially.\\nForm the predicted target directly from the fetch PC and instruction immediate,\\nqualify prediction on io_imemReady, and carry only the predicted next PC through\\nID into EX. EX compares that value with the resolved architectural next PC and\\nredirects on either a false-taken or missed-taken prediction; JALR remains\\nresolved in EX. With no entries, tags, counters, target array, or update path,\\nthis should recover much of the observed loop/call cycle reduction while adding\\nonly immediate wiring, one target adder, and a small opcode/direction mux to the\\nfetch path, greatly limiting the Fmax and LUT penalty that defeated the BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a predicted-next-PC field to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so EX can verify the static fetch decision without any predictor table state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode only BRANCH and JAL opcodes from the ready fetched word, construct their immediates, predict backward conditional branches and all direct JALs taken, and select the corresponding next fetch PC while preserving redirect and stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the predicted next PC alongside the instruction while leaving decoder legality and architectural operands unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the resolved architectural next PC, redirect only when it differs from the carried prediction, and retain existing misalignment-trap, divider, RVFI pc_wdata, and memory-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the prediction-aware EX recovery signal through the existing redirect and flush path without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed backward-branch, forward-branch, JAL, JALR, misprediction-recovery, and randomized bus-stall cases that check exact retirement order and architectural next PCs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a predicted-next-PC field to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so EX can verify the static fetch decision without any predictor table state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode only BRANCH and JAL opcodes from the ready fetched word, construct their immediates, predict backward conditional branches and all direct JALs taken, and select the corresponding next fetch PC while preserving redirect and stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the predicted next PC alongside the instruction while leaving decoder legality and architectural operands unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the resolved architectural next PC, redirect only when it differs from the carried prediction, and retain existing misalignment-trap, divider, RVFI pc_wdata, and memory-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the prediction-aware EX recovery signal through the existing redirect and flush path without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed backward-branch, forward-branch, JAL, JALR, misprediction-recovery, and randomized bus-stall cases that check exact retirement order and architectural next PCs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 379.02, \"delta_pct\": -0.2, \"vs_baseline\": 34.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 161.45, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5795, \"ff\": 2232, \"seeds\": [159.24, 162.79, 161.45], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:10:04.779961Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -0.20%): Even table-free backward-branch/JAL prediction is slightly net-negative when immediate decode, a 32-bit target adder, and a taken mux remain combinational on the fetch-PC feedback path, so avoiding EX redirect bubbles requires a decoupled or pipelined target path.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Collapse redundant execute-stage target adders\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The execute stage currently builds branch targets with a dedicated PC-plus-immediate\\nadder and JALR targets with another rs1-plus-immediate adder, while the main ALU also\\nperforms an unused ADD for those same control-flow instructions. These parallel\\n32-bit adders and their routing consume LUTs and placement freedom on the redirect\\npath, and branch metadata is then clocked into EX/MEM even though no later stage uses it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The execute stage currently builds branch targets with a dedicated PC-plus-immediate\\nadder and JALR targets with another rs1-plus-immediate adder, while the main ALU also\\nperforms an unused ADD for those same control-flow instructions. These parallel\\n32-bit adders and their routing consume LUTs and placement freedom on the redirect\\npath, and branch metadata is then clocked into EX/MEM even though no later stage uses it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Select PC and the decoded immediate as the main ALU operands for conditional branches\\nand direct JAL, and select forwarded rs1 and the immediate for JALR, allowing the ALU's\\nexisting ADD result to serve as the sole control-flow target sum. Apply JALR bit-zero\\nclearing after that shared result, while leaving the separate comparators, redirect\\nrules, misalignment traps, PC-plus-four link value, and dmem-stall suppression intact.\\nRemove the dead branch_taken and branch_target fields from EX/MEM so they no longer\\nconsume flops or routing. Eliminating redundant adders and payload should improve\\nmedian FPGA Fmax with no CoreMark cycle penalty and no change to architectural state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route branch, JAL, and JALR target arithmetic through the existing ALU ADD path; preserve forwarding, JALR alignment, redirect gating, traps, link writes, divider interlocks, and RVFI pc_wdata behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target members from ex_mem_t to reduce EX/MEM register width and downstream routing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update the EX/MEM payload usage annotations after pruning dead branch metadata, without changing memory or RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed taken and not-taken branches plus JAL and forwarded JALR cases, including misaligned targets and bus stalls, to verify shared target arithmetic and exact RVFI next PCs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route branch, JAL, and JALR target arithmetic through the existing ALU ADD path; preserve forwarding, JALR alignment, redirect gating, traps, link writes, divider interlocks, and RVFI pc_wdata behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the unused branch_taken and branch_target members from ex_mem_t to reduce EX/MEM register width and downstream routing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Update the EX/MEM payload usage annotations after pruning dead branch metadata, without changing memory or RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed taken and not-taken branches plus JAL and forwarded JALR cases, including misaligned targets and bus stalls, to verify shared target arithmetic and exact RVFI next PCs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 396.24, \"delta_pct\": -4.03, \"vs_baseline\": 40.1, \"fmax_mhz\": 169.89, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4287593, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5620, \"ff\": 2200, \"seeds\": [166.58, 169.89, 173.25], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:22:21.346402Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r3s0 (regression, -4.03%): Sharing the main ALU adder for branch/JAL/JALR targets can worsen FPGA fitness because control-flow operand selects and the ALU result mux move onto the redirect path, outweighing removal of dedicated target adders and dead EX/MEM metadata.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch with a two-entry elastic instruction queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end freezes its fetch PC whenever dmem or the iterative\\ndivider stalls the back end, even when instruction memory is ready. Those\\nready cycles are discarded, and subsequent independent random iStalls inject\\nbubbles directly into the pipeline. The failed predictors also show that\\nadding combinational decode, target arithmetic, or table lookup to the fetch-PC\\nfeedback path costs too much Fmax on this FPGA.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end freezes its fetch PC whenever dmem or the iterative\\ndivider stalls the back end, even when instruction memory is ready. Those\\nready cycles are discarded, and subsequent independent random iStalls inject\\nbubbles directly into the pipeline. The failed predictors also show that\\nadding combinational decode, target arithmetic, or table lookup to the fetch-PC\\nfeedback path costs too much Fmax on this FPGA.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF-to-ID handoff with a two-entry elastic instruction\\nqueue and separate fetch and decode PCs. While ID is backpressured by dmem or a\\nlong divide, continue accepting sequential imem responses until the queue is\\nfull; when ID advances, pop one entry and simultaneously refill whenever\\nio_imemReady is asserted. A queued instruction can therefore feed ID during a\\nlater imem-not-ready cycle, converting overlap between independent bus stalls\\ninto fewer pipeline bubbles. EX redirects atomically invalidate both entries\\nand restart the fetch PC at the resolved target, so no wrong-path instruction\\ncan retire. The queue state and addresses are registered, leaving only shallow\\nvalid/full control on the sequential fetch path and avoiding the critical-path\\npenalty observed for branch prediction.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry fall-through instruction queue with independent fetch-PC advancement, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, full backpressure, and redirect-priority invalidation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate ID consumption backpressure from instruction-fetch progress so dmem and divider stalls hold ID while fetch continues only until the elastic queue reports full.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire queue valid/consume/full handshakes between IF, ID, and hazard control while preserving the nret=1 interface and existing retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized tests proving the queue hides independent iStalls after back-end stalls, preserves one-copy in-order retirement, and discards all queued wrong-path entries on redirects.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry fall-through instruction queue with independent fetch-PC advancement, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, full backpressure, and redirect-priority invalidation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate ID consumption backpressure from instruction-fetch progress so dmem and divider stalls hold ID while fetch continues only until the elastic queue reports full.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire queue valid/consume/full handshakes between IF, ID, and hazard control while preserving the nret=1 interface and existing retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized tests proving the queue hides independent iStalls after back-end stalls, preserves one-copy in-order retirement, and discards all queued wrong-path entries on redirects.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 317.89, \"delta_pct\": -23.0, \"vs_baseline\": 12.4, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.07, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4563552, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5877, \"ff\": 2360, \"seeds\": [153.54, 139.72, 145.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:26:56.628785Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -23.00%): A registered-only two-entry fetch queue adds an empty refill cycle after every EX redirect, so CoreMark's increased control-flow recovery penalty can overwhelm the limited benefit of overlapping independent imem stalls with dmem/divider backpressure unless the queue has fall-through bypass or prediction.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny return-address stack for canonical returns\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The two broader predictors reduced control-flow bubbles but lost fitness because\\nBTB lookup or immediate target generation lengthened the fetch-PC feedback path.\\nCoreMark still pays the full EX-stage redirect penalty on every function return,\\nwhile a return target is already known when its call executes and can be retained\\nin a few registers without tags, counters, or a target adder at fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two broader predictors reduced control-flow bubbles but lost fitness because\\nBTB lookup or immediate target generation lengthened the fetch-PC feedback path.\\nCoreMark still pays the full EX-stage redirect penalty on every function return,\\nwhile a return target is already known when its call executes and can be retained\\nin a few registers without tags, counters, or a target adder at fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a four-entry return-address stack that pushes PC+4 when a legal call writing\\nx1 advances through EX and pops when a canonical RET (JALR x0,x1,0) advances.\\nIn fetch, recognize only that canonical RET and, when the stack is nonempty and\\nimem is ready, select the registered stack top as predicted next PC. Carry the\\npredicted next PC with the instruction and let EX redirect only if the resolved\\narchitectural next PC differs, so stale, underflowed, or unusual JALR cases recover\\nnormally. This targets frequent function returns while keeping the added feedback\\npath to a narrow instruction predecode and mux, avoiding the target adder and the\\nindexed tag/counter arrays that caused both earlier predictor regressions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/return_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a four-entry return-address stack with EX-accept-qualified call pushes and canonical-return pops, exposing only a registered top target and nonempty flag.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so return predictions can be verified in EX without changing architectural RVFI fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize canonical RET instructions, qualify prediction on imem readiness and stack validity, and select the precomputed stack-top target without an immediate adder or table lookup.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch return-prediction metadata alongside the instruction while preserving default-illegal decode and source operand reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare carried predicted next PC with the resolved JALR next PC, recover on mismatch, and emit stall-qualified call/return events for precise stack updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the return predictor, prediction recovery, and update signals through the existing redirect/flush path without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add nested-call, canonical-return, stack-underflow, noncanonical-JALR, misprediction-recovery, and randomized bus-stall tests checking exact retirement and RVFI next PCs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/return_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a four-entry return-address stack with EX-accept-qualified call pushes and canonical-return pops, exposing only a registered top target and nonempty flag.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so return predictions can be verified in EX without changing architectural RVFI fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize canonical RET instructions, qualify prediction on imem readiness and stack validity, and select the precomputed stack-top target without an immediate adder or table lookup.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch return-prediction metadata alongside the instruction while preserving default-illegal decode and source operand reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare carried predicted next PC with the resolved JALR next PC, recover on mismatch, and emit stall-qualified call/return events for precise stack updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the return predictor, prediction recovery, and update signals through the existing redirect/flush path without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add nested-call, canonical-return, stack-underflow, noncanonical-JALR, misprediction-recovery, and randomized bus-stall tests checking exact retirement and RVFI next PCs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 336.83, \"delta_pct\": -18.42, \"vs_baseline\": 19.1, \"fmax_mhz\": 143.97, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4274274, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5906, \"ff\": 2357, \"seeds\": [133.55, 143.97, 156.03], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:28:26.162687Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -18.42%): Even a tag-free four-entry RAS regresses fitness when canonical-RET predecode and a 32-bit ras_top mux remain combinational in the fetch-PC feedback path, so return prediction must be decoupled or staged rather than selected directly from imem_data.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Fall-through fetch buffer with zero redirect refill penalty\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The previous two-entry fetch queue regressed because its registered-only\\noutput forced an empty refill cycle after every EX redirect. However, the\\nunderlying opportunity remains: dmem and divider stalls currently stop\\ninstruction fetch even when imem is ready, wasting bus-ready cycles that\\ncould cover later random iStalls. A fall-through design can capture that\\noverlap while retaining the baseline front-end latency on the common empty\\nand redirect-recovery paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous two-entry fetch queue regressed because its registered-only\\noutput forced an empty refill cycle after every EX redirect. However, the\\nunderlying opportunity remains: dmem and divider stalls currently stop\\ninstruction fetch even when imem is ready, wasting bus-ready cycles that\\ncould cover later random iStalls. A fall-through design can capture that\\noverlap while retaining the baseline front-end latency on the common empty\\nand redirect-recovery paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the IF handoff with a two-entry fall-through elastic buffer whose\\nempty state feeds the current imem response directly to ID in the same\\ncycle. Storage is used only when ID is backpressured or when a queued entry\\nremains; simultaneous dequeue and enqueue sustain one instruction per\\nready cycle. An EX redirect clears both entries, sets the fetch PC to the\\nresolved target, and allows the target response to fall through on the very\\nnext cycle, exactly matching the baseline redirect latency rather than\\npaying a queue-refill bubble. This should overlap independent imem-ready\\ncycles with dmem/divider stalls and reduce CoreMark cycles without adding\\ndecode, target arithmetic, or predictor lookup to the fetch-PC feedback\\npath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry fall-through elastic instruction buffer with direct empty bypass, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, independent sequential fetch advancement while ID is blocked, and redirect-priority clearing that preserves baseline target refill latency.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate ID backpressure from fetch-buffer backpressure so dmem and divider stalls hold ID while fetch continues until the buffer is full, without changing redirect priority or memory-stall correctness.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire explicit fetch-valid, ID-consume, and buffer-full handshakes while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, retirement boundary, and RVFI ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-exact tests for empty-buffer fall-through and redirect recovery, plus randomized imem/dmem stalls proving no duplicate, dropped, or wrong-path retirement and demonstrating that target fetch reaches ID with no extra refill cycle.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry fall-through elastic instruction buffer with direct empty bypass, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, independent sequential fetch advancement while ID is blocked, and redirect-priority clearing that preserves baseline target refill latency.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate ID backpressure from fetch-buffer backpressure so dmem and divider stalls hold ID while fetch continues until the buffer is full, without changing redirect priority or memory-stall correctness.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire explicit fetch-valid, ID-consume, and buffer-full handshakes while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, retirement boundary, and RVFI ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-exact tests for empty-buffer fall-through and redirect recovery, plus randomized imem/dmem stalls proving no duplicate, dropped, or wrong-path retirement and demonstrating that target fetch reaches ID with no extra refill cycle.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Lookahead registered branch target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Earlier predictors reduced control-flow recovery cycles, but each placed\\ninstruction predecode, target arithmetic, or a table lookup directly on the\\nfetch-PC feedback path and lost more Fmax than it gained in IPC. The normal\\nsequential fetch stream reveals PC+4 one cycle before that instruction is\\nfetched, providing an opportunity to perform predictor lookup ahead of time\\nand register its result away from the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Earlier predictors reduced control-flow recovery cycles, but each placed\\ninstruction predecode, target arithmetic, or a table lookup directly on the\\nfetch-PC feedback path and lost more Fmax than it gained in IPC. The normal\\nsequential fetch stream reveals PC+4 one cycle before that instruction is\\nfetched, providing an opportunity to perform predictor lookup ahead of time\\nand register its result away from the PC critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit direction counters, but\\nlook it up using the next sequential fetch address rather than the currently\\nfetched address. When an instruction fetch is accepted, latch the hit,\\npredicted target, and predicted-taken result for PC+4; on the following\\ncycle those registered signals are already aligned with that PC and select\\nits next address without tag comparison, instruction decoding, target\\naddition, or array data on the PC feedback path. Carry the prediction with\\nthe instruction and let EX recover whenever the resolved architectural next\\nPC differs. Invalidate the lookahead result across redirects and nonaccepted\\nfetches, and train only from valid control-flow instructions that actually\\nadvance through EX. This should retain most of the BTB's control-flow cycle\\nsavings while avoiding the combinational fetch loop that caused every prior\\npredictor regression.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small tagged BTB with direction counters, combinational PC+4 lookahead lookup, registered aligned prediction output, redirect invalidation, and EX-accept-qualified training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can verify each registered lookahead prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only registered predictor hit and target signals in the next-PC mux, maintain prediction-to-fetch alignment across imem stalls, and invalidate lookahead state on redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata with the fetched instruction while preserving decoder legality and architectural source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the carried predicted next PC against the resolved architectural next PC, recover in both prediction directions, and emit stall-qualified BTB training information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the lookahead predictor and connect registered prediction, recovery, invalidation, and training signals without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, BTB alias, taken/not-taken transition, redirect warmup, and randomized imem/dmem stall tests that verify exact RVFI retirement and recovery.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small tagged BTB with direction counters, combinational PC+4 lookahead lookup, registered aligned prediction output, redirect invalidation, and EX-accept-qualified training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry predicted-next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can verify each registered lookahead prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only registered predictor hit and target signals in the next-PC mux, maintain prediction-to-fetch alignment across imem stalls, and invalidate lookahead state on redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata with the fetched instruction while preserving decoder legality and architectural source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the carried predicted next PC against the resolved architectural next PC, recover in both prediction directions, and emit stall-qualified BTB training information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the lookahead predictor and connect registered prediction, recovery, invalidation, and training signals without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, BTB alias, taken/not-taken transition, redirect warmup, and randomized imem/dmem stall tests that verify exact RVFI retirement and recovery.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 259.17, \"delta_pct\": -37.23, \"vs_baseline\": -8.36, \"fmax_mhz\": 106.26, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4099943, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 8102, \"ff\": 3240, \"seeds\": [107.08, 106.26, 104.6], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:57:00.059328Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -37.23%): Registering a lookahead BTB result does not guarantee timing decoupling when the same cycle still runs fetch PC through a PC+4 adder, tagged table lookup, and direction logic into those registers; that reg-to-reg cone can replace the fetch feedback loop as the Fmax limiter.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Replace load barrel shift with lane muxes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The successful MEM-to-EX load bypass made load extraction part of a long\\nsame-cycle path from io_dmemRData through forwarding and the consumer ALU or\\nbranch comparator into EX/MEM. The current generic 32-bit variable right\\nshift moves far more data than an RV32 byte or halfword load needs and may\\nconsume both LUT depth and routing on this now performance-critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful MEM-to-EX load bypass made load extraction part of a long\\nsame-cycle path from io_dmemRData through forwarding and the consumer ALU or\\nbranch comparator into EX/MEM. The current generic 32-bit variable right\\nshift moves far more data than an RV32 byte or halfword load needs and may\\nconsume both LUT depth and routing on this now performance-critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the generic dmem_rdata shift with width-specific direct lane selects:\\na four-way 8-bit mux for byte loads, a two-way 16-bit mux for aligned\\nhalfword loads, and an unmodified word path. Perform sign or zero extension\\nonly after the narrow mux, and express store masks with explicit address\\ncases rather than variable shifts. This preserves every load value, memory\\nmask, misalignment trap, bypass qualification, and pipeline cycle while\\nreducing the combinational depth and switched routing between memory and EX,\\nso median Fmax should improve without an IPC penalty.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the generic 32-bit load shift and variable store-mask shift with narrow explicit byte/halfword lane muxes and address-case masks, preserving alignment traps, RVFI fields, and the same-cycle load bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed LB/LBU cases at every byte offset and LH/LHU cases at both aligned halfword offsets, including adjacent dependent consumers and dmem stalls, to verify lane selection, extension, bypass timing, and RVFI masks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the generic 32-bit load shift and variable store-mask shift with narrow explicit byte/halfword lane muxes and address-case masks, preserving alignment traps, RVFI fields, and the same-cycle load bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed LB/LBU cases at every byte offset and LH/LHU cases at both aligned halfword offsets, including adjacent dependent consumers and dmem stalls, to verify lane selection, extension, bypass timing, and RVFI masks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 381.22, \"delta_pct\": -7.67, \"vs_baseline\": 34.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 163.45, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4287593, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5730, \"ff\": 2200, \"seeds\": [163.45, 155.28, 166.22], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:07:50.951443Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Retime PC-relative targets ahead of execute\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The frequent conditional branches in CoreMark currently place a 32-bit\\nPC-plus-immediate adder in the EX-to-fetch redirect cone. Prior predictor\\nattempts show that target arithmetic and selection on a fetch-PC path are\\nparticularly expensive on this FPGA, while sharing more logic into the main\\nEX adder also regressed. The PC and immediate are already available one stage\\nearlier, so this work need not remain in the redirect stage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The frequent conditional branches in CoreMark currently place a 32-bit\\nPC-plus-immediate adder in the EX-to-fetch redirect cone. Prior predictor\\nattempts show that target arithmetic and selection on a fetch-PC path are\\nparticularly expensive on this FPGA, while sharing more logic into the main\\nEX adder also regressed. The PC and immediate are already available one stage\\nearlier, so this work need not remain in the redirect stage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute PC-relative targets for BRANCH and JAL in decode and register the\\nresult in ID/EX. EX will use that registered target for branch comparison,\\nredirect, misalignment checking, RVFI pc_wdata, and captured metadata, while\\nretaining the existing forwarded rs1-plus-immediate path for JALR. This\\nretimes the dedicated 32-bit target adder across the ID/EX boundary and\\nshortens the common branch redirect path without changing branch penalties,\\nretirement timing, prediction state, or the nret=1 interface. The decode\\ntarget computation is independent of regfile data, so it should trade a\\nrelatively uncongested ID cone for higher median Fmax on the control-critical\\nEX-to-PC feedback path with no CoreMark cycle increase.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative target field to the ID/EX payload, without changing any architectural or RVFI-visible field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute PC plus the decoded B/J immediate and latch it into ID/EX only when the stage advances, preserving reset, flush, and stall semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered target for conditional branches and JAL in redirect, alignment, pc_next, and saved metadata paths; keep forwarded operand addition for JALR and all existing divider/backpressure behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed forward/backward branch and JAL cases across randomized stalls, including misaligned targets, to verify redirects, traps, RVFI pc_wdata, and one-copy retirement after the retiming.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative target field to the ID/EX payload, without changing any architectural or RVFI-visible field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute PC plus the decoded B/J immediate and latch it into ID/EX only when the stage advances, preserving reset, flush, and stall semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered target for conditional branches and JAL in redirect, alignment, pc_next, and saved metadata paths; keep forwarded operand addition for JALR and all existing divider/backpressure behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed forward/backward branch and JAL cases across randomized stalls, including misaligned targets, to verify redirects, traps, RVFI pc_wdata, and one-copy retirement after the retiming.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.58, \"delta_pct\": -16.3, \"vs_baseline\": 22.19, \"fmax_mhz\": 148.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4287593, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5664, \"ff\": 2232, \"seeds\": [155.26, 146.03, 148.17], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:12:39.104809Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r5s1 (regression, -16.30%): Retiming the branch/JAL PC-plus-immediate adder into decode can worsen FPGA fitness because the always-active 32-bit sum and added ID/EX target routing outweigh shortening the EX redirect cone when CoreMark's branch penalty and cycle count are unchanged.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"JAL-only exact static predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The combined backward-branch and JAL static predictor cut CoreMark cycles by\\nabout five percent and finished only 0.20 percent below its then-best fitness,\\nwhile every predictor with tables, tags, counters, or a return stack lost far\\nmore Fmax. Its remaining cost included conditional-branch classification and\\ndirection logic, two immediate formats, and a full predicted-PC value carried\\nthrough the front end. Direct JAL is a narrower opportunity: it is always\\ntaken and its target is encoded in the fetched instruction, so it needs no\\nlearned state and cannot suffer a direction misprediction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The combined backward-branch and JAL static predictor cut CoreMark cycles by\\nabout five percent and finished only 0.20 percent below its then-best fitness,\\nwhile every predictor with tables, tags, counters, or a return stack lost far\\nmore Fmax. Its remaining cost included conditional-branch classification and\\ndirection logic, two immediate formats, and a full predicted-PC value carried\\nthrough the front end. Direct JAL is a narrower opportunity: it is always\\ntaken and its target is encoded in the fetched instruction, so it needs no\\nlearned state and cannot suffer a direction misprediction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict only aligned JAL instructions in fetch. Recognize the JAL opcode,\\nconstruct its J immediate, and select PC plus that immediate as the next fetch\\naddress when the instruction response is accepted. Carry a single\\njal_predicted bit with the instruction rather than a 32-bit predicted target;\\nEX recomputes the architectural target as it already does and suppresses its\\nredirect only for a valid, aligned JAL carrying that bit. All conditional\\nbranches and JALR remain predict-not-taken and use the unchanged EX recovery\\npath. This preserves the direct-call and unconditional-jump cycle savings of\\nthe nearly neutral table-free experiment while removing its B-immediate\\ndecode, backward-branch policy, false-taken recovery comparisons, and wide\\nprediction metadata, giving the reduced fetch logic a better chance to retain\\nthe current 177 MHz median Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only a one-bit JAL-predicted flag to IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, avoiding the prior predictor's 32-bit predicted-next-PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement accepted-response JAL-only predecode, J-immediate target addition, aligned-target qualification, and predicted next-PC selection while preserving redirect priority and all imem stall semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit JAL prediction flag with its instruction without changing decoder legality, source operands, or stall and flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the normal EX redirect only for an exactly matched aligned JAL prediction; retain existing branch and JALR redirects, misalignment traps, divider interlock, RVFI pc_wdata, and dmem-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the narrow prediction metadata and prediction-aware redirect through the existing nret=1 pipeline without changing top-level IO or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed forward and backward JAL, x0-link JAL, misaligned-target, redirect, and randomized bus-stall cases proving correct targets, no duplicate or wrong-path retirement, and unchanged RVFI ordering.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only a one-bit JAL-predicted flag to IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, avoiding the prior predictor's 32-bit predicted-next-PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement accepted-response JAL-only predecode, J-immediate target addition, aligned-target qualification, and predicted next-PC selection while preserving redirect priority and all imem stall semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit JAL prediction flag with its instruction without changing decoder legality, source operands, or stall and flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the normal EX redirect only for an exactly matched aligned JAL prediction; retain existing branch and JALR redirects, misalignment traps, divider interlock, RVFI pc_wdata, and dmem-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the narrow prediction metadata and prediction-aware redirect through the existing nret=1 pipeline without changing top-level IO or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed forward and backward JAL, x0-link JAL, misaligned-target, redirect, and randomized bus-stall cases proving correct targets, no duplicate or wrong-path retirement, and unchanged RVFI ordering.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 377.71, \"delta_pct\": -8.52, \"vs_baseline\": 33.55, \"fmax_mhz\": 160.1, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4238664, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5786, \"ff\": 2201, \"seeds\": [158.78, 161.26, 160.1], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:10:34.436415Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Narrow post-execute pipeline metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The full decode control bundle is clocked through both EX/MEM and MEM/WB even\\nthough most of its fields become dead after execute. EX/MEM also retains two\\nbranch-result fields that no downstream stage reads. These unnecessary flops\\nand high-fanout routes consume placement freedom around the already dense\\nexecute and memory stages without contributing to CoreMark throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The full decode control bundle is clocked through both EX/MEM and MEM/WB even\\nthough most of its fields become dead after execute. EX/MEM also retains two\\nbranch-result fields that no downstream stage reads. These unnecessary flops\\nand high-fanout routes consume placement freedom around the already dense\\nexecute and memory stages without contributing to CoreMark throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce stage-specific post-EX and post-MEM control payloads and retain only\\nthe bits consumed downstream: memory access controls in EX/MEM, and writeback\\nplus trap controls in MEM/WB. Remove the unused registered branch-taken and\\nbranch-target metadata while leaving the existing dedicated target adders and\\nredirect datapath unchanged. Unlike the earlier experiment that combined\\nmetadata pruning with a slower shared control-flow adder, this isolates the\\nregister and routing reduction. CoreMark cycles should remain identical while\\nthe smaller pipeline registers and reduced control fanout modestly improve\\nplacement and median Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define narrow EX/MEM and MEM/WB control payloads, use them in the corresponding bundles, and remove downstream-unused branch_taken and branch_target fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM for normal and divider-completion paths while preserving all arithmetic, redirect, alignment, stall, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM controls and emit only writeback and trap controls into MEM/WB, preserving memory masks, load bypass, misalignment traps, and held-WB semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard and forwarding control references for the narrowed bundles without changing the nret=1 interface, bus handshakes, or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the narrowed MEM/WB writeback controls with unchanged register-file write qualification and result selection.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed ALU, load/store, illegal-instruction, branch/jump, divider, dependency, and randomized bus-stall coverage to prove metadata pruning is cycle- and RVFI-transparent.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define narrow EX/MEM and MEM/WB control payloads, use them in the corresponding bundles, and remove downstream-unused branch_taken and branch_target fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM for normal and divider-completion paths while preserving all arithmetic, redirect, alignment, stall, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM controls and emit only writeback and trap controls into MEM/WB, preserving memory masks, load bypass, misalignment traps, and held-WB semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard and forwarding control references for the narrowed bundles without changing the nret=1 interface, bus handshakes, or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the narrowed MEM/WB writeback controls with unchanged register-file write qualification and result selection.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed ALU, load/store, illegal-instruction, branch/jump, divider, dependency, and randomized bus-stall coverage to prove metadata pruning is cycle- and RVFI-transparent.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 366.66, \"delta_pct\": -11.19, \"vs_baseline\": 29.64, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4287593, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5721, \"ff\": 2200, \"seeds\": [152.04, 157.21, 160.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:23:16.139605Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Same-page short-loop branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The table-free backward-branch predictor produced the best predictor result so\\nfar and reduced CoreMark cycles, but its full-width target adder on the fetch-PC\\npath erased the IPC gain through lower Fmax. CoreMark loop backedges are often\\nshort, so many useful predictions should not require a 32-bit target addition.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The table-free backward-branch predictor produced the best predictor result so\\nfar and reduced CoreMark cycles, but its full-width target adder on the fetch-PC\\npath erased the IPC gain through lower Fmax. CoreMark loop backedges are often\\nshort, so many useful predictions should not require a 32-bit target addition.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict only conditional backward branches whose displacement is in the short\\nrange -256 through -2 and whose target remains in the current 256-byte PC page.\\nForm the target with a 9-bit low-PC plus signed-displacement addition; the ninth\\nsum bit both proves that the negative displacement did not borrow across the\\npage and qualifies the prediction, while the upper 24 PC bits pass through\\nunchanged. Carry only a one-bit predicted-taken flag into EX. A correctly taken\\nprediction suppresses the normal redirect, while a not-taken outcome redirects\\nto PC+4; every unqualified branch retains the existing predict-not-taken path.\\nThis captures compact hot loops without predictor state, tags, wide metadata,\\nor a full-width fetch target adder, offering the cycle benefit of the closest\\nprior predictor with a much smaller Fmax penalty.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit branch-predicted-taken field to IF/ID and ID/EX, avoiding wide predicted-PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize only short negative conditional branches, use a 9-bit low-PC target sum with same-page qualification, and select the narrow predicted target while preserving redirect and stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction flag with its instruction under the existing reset, flush, and stall rules without changing decode legality or source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Verify the predicted branch outcome, suppress redirect on a correctly predicted taken branch, and recover to PC+4 on false-taken predictions while preserving misalignment traps, RVFI pc_wdata, forwarding, divider, and dmem-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the narrow prediction metadata and bidirectional EX recovery through the existing nret=1 redirect and flush path without changing top-level IO or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add taken and not-taken short backward branches, same-page boundary rejection, longer-backedge fallback, false-taken recovery, and randomized bus-stall cases checking exact RVFI retirement and next PCs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit branch-predicted-taken field to IF/ID and ID/EX, avoiding wide predicted-PC metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize only short negative conditional branches, use a 9-bit low-PC target sum with same-page qualification, and select the narrow predicted target while preserving redirect and stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction flag with its instruction under the existing reset, flush, and stall rules without changing decode legality or source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Verify the predicted branch outcome, suppress redirect on a correctly predicted taken branch, and recover to PC+4 on false-taken predictions while preserving misalignment traps, RVFI pc_wdata, forwarding, divider, and dmem-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the narrow prediction metadata and bidirectional EX recovery through the existing nret=1 redirect and flush path without changing top-level IO or retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add taken and not-taken short backward branches, same-page boundary rejection, longer-backedge fallback, false-taken recovery, and randomized bus-stall cases checking exact RVFI retirement and next PCs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 196.19, \"delta_pct\": -52.48, \"vs_baseline\": -30.63, \"fmax_mhz\": 80.43, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4099590, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5796, \"ff\": 2195, \"seeds\": [79.6, 80.43, 83.43], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:27:19.089385Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -52.48%): Narrowing a backward-branch target calculation to a same-page 9-bit add does not rescue fetch timing when B-immediate legality/range decode and the predicted-target mux still form an instruction-dependent PC feedback cone, so short-loop prediction must be staged or decoupled rather than merely width-reduced.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Cold-path high-half multiply operations\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The common execute path still contains three separately described 64-bit\\nproducts for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU. CoreMark needs ordinary low-half\\nMUL but is not expected to execute the three high-half variants inside the\\ntimed region. The earlier attempt to algebraically share one combinational\\nproduct regressed because its sign-correction subtractors enlarged the same\\none-cycle path; moving only the cold variants out of that path avoids that\\ncorrection cone while preserving common-case multiply throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The common execute path still contains three separately described 64-bit\\nproducts for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU. CoreMark needs ordinary low-half\\nMUL but is not expected to execute the three high-half variants inside the\\ntimed region. The earlier attempt to algebraically share one combinational\\nproduct regressed because its sign-correction subtractors enlarged the same\\none-cycle path; moving only the cold variants out of that path avoids that\\ncorrection cone while preserving common-case multiply throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep RV32M MUL as the existing single-cycle low-half product, but implement\\nMULH, MULHU, and MULHSU in one shared iterative shift-add unit. On a high-half\\nmultiply, EX captures the post-forwarding operands and complete instruction\\npayload, holds younger stages through the existing long-operation interlock,\\nemits bubbles while the unit runs, and advances the result exactly once.\\nSignedness is handled by operand normalization and final 64-bit sign restore,\\nwith the architectural result taken from bits 63:32. This removes two cold\\n64-bit multiplier structures and their result-selection routing from the\\nordinary ALU while leaving CoreMark's single-cycle MUL latency and cycle count\\nunchanged, so reduced DSP/LUT routing pressure should raise median Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mulh_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative 32-step shift-add engine for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU with signed operand normalization, 64-bit sign restoration, a one-shot start/busy/done protocol, and a shallow RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain only the single-cycle low-half MUL product in the normal ALU and remove combinational MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU products, while preserving all ALTOPS opcode results.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route high-half multiply instructions through the new unit, capture forwarded operands and RVFI payload at launch, reuse the long-operation hold discipline, and emit one completed EX/MEM result across dmem backpressure without affecting ordinary MUL or DIV/REM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the existing EX long-operation interlock, if needed, so high-half multiply and divide completion share the same redirect and bus-stall priorities without replaying or dropping instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the generalized long-operation busy path while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, single retirement channel, and strict RVFI order.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep direct single-cycle MUL coverage and verify that high-half operations no longer depend on combinational ALU outputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add signed and unsigned high-half multiply dependencies, back-to-back long operations, and randomized imem/dmem stall cases checking exact results, bounded progress, single retirement, and RVFI operand data.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mulh_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative 32-step shift-add engine for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU with signed operand normalization, 64-bit sign restoration, a one-shot start/busy/done protocol, and a shallow RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain only the single-cycle low-half MUL product in the normal ALU and remove combinational MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU products, while preserving all ALTOPS opcode results.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route high-half multiply instructions through the new unit, capture forwarded operands and RVFI payload at launch, reuse the long-operation hold discipline, and emit one completed EX/MEM result across dmem backpressure without affecting ordinary MUL or DIV/REM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the existing EX long-operation interlock, if needed, so high-half multiply and divide completion share the same redirect and bus-stall priorities without replaying or dropping instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the generalized long-operation busy path while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, single retirement channel, and strict RVFI order.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep direct single-cycle MUL coverage and verify that high-half operations no longer depend on combinational ALU outputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add signed and unsigned high-half multiply dependencies, back-to-back long operations, and randomized imem/dmem stall cases checking exact results, bounded progress, single retirement, and RVFI operand data.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 315.0, \"delta_pct\": -23.7, \"vs_baseline\": 11.38, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.06, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4287561, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6127, \"ff\": 2397, \"seeds\": [135.06, 143.8, 132.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:38:25.043948Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:22:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Single-entry registered loop-target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Taken loop backedges still pay the full execute-stage redirect penalty, but\\nevery prior predictor placed instruction predecode, target arithmetic, or an\\nindexed table on the fetch-PC path and lost more Fmax than it saved in cycles.\\nA single learned loop entry can reuse an already-resolved target with much\\nless lookup and routing logic than those unsuccessful designs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Taken loop backedges still pay the full execute-stage redirect penalty, but\\nevery prior predictor placed instruction predecode, target arithmetic, or an\\nindexed table on the fetch-PC path and lost more Fmax than it saved in cycles.\\nA single learned loop entry can reuse an already-resolved target with much\\nless lookup and routing logic than those unsuccessful designs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one fully registered loop-target entry containing the exact branch PC and\\nits resolved backward target. Train it only from valid, taken conditional\\nbranches that advance through EX; fetch predicts taken only on an exact PC\\nmatch, so its feedback path is limited to one equality reduction and a mux\\nbetween registered target and PC+4, with no imem-data decode, immediate\\nconstruction, target adder, indexed array, or direction counter. Carry the\\npredicted next PC with the instruction and make EX redirect whenever it\\ndiffers from the resolved architectural next PC, including the one loop-exit\\nfalse prediction and any stale entry. This should capture repeated hot-loop\\nbackedges while keeping the predictor small enough that its cycle reduction\\ncan outweigh its modest timing cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/loop_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single registered exact-PC loop-target entry, lookup from fetch PC, and stall-qualified training from taken backward conditional branches in EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry prediction-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX for exact stale-target and loop-exit recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered loop target only on an exact predictor hit while preserving redirect priority, imem acceptance semantics, and sequential PC+4 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch predictor metadata with the fetched instruction under the existing reset, flush, and stall rules without changing decoder legality or RVFI source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare carried predicted next PC with the resolved architectural next PC, recover on false or stale predictions, and emit training only when a valid conditional branch advances.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the one-entry predictor and wire lookup, recovery, and stall-qualified training through the existing nret=1 pipeline and redirect path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, loop-exit, entry-replacement, stale-target, and randomized bus-stall tests checking wrong-path suppression, RVFI PCs, order, and forward progress.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/loop_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single registered exact-PC loop-target entry, lookup from fetch PC, and stall-qualified training from taken backward conditional branches in EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry prediction-valid and predicted-next-PC metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX for exact stale-target and loop-exit recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered loop target only on an exact predictor hit while preserving redirect priority, imem acceptance semantics, and sequential PC+4 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch predictor metadata with the fetched instruction under the existing reset, flush, and stall rules without changing decoder legality or RVFI source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare carried predicted next PC with the resolved architectural next PC, recover on false or stale predictions, and emit training only when a valid conditional branch advances.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the one-entry predictor and wire lookup, recovery, and stall-qualified training through the existing nret=1 pipeline and redirect path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, loop-exit, entry-replacement, stale-target, and randomized bus-stall tests checking wrong-path suppression, RVFI PCs, order, and forward progress.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 212.65, \"delta_pct\": -48.49, \"vs_baseline\": -24.81, \"fmax_mhz\": 87.38, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4109157, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5848, \"ff\": 2297, \"seeds\": [85.03, 87.38, 97.1], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:39:58.289519Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -48.49%): Even a single fully registered loop-target entry leaves a 32-bit exact-PC compare and predicted-target mux in the fetch-PC recurrence, so eliminating predecode, target arithmetic, and table indexing is insufficient unless prediction lookup is also decoupled from next-PC selection.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"One-entry fall-through instruction skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The pipeline currently freezes the fetch PC whenever dmem or the iterative\\ndivider blocks decode, even when imem is ready. Those otherwise useful fetch\\ncycles cannot hide a later random imem stall. The registered fetch queue\\nexperiment lost cycles by requiring refill after redirects, while the prior\\nfall-through attempt failed cosimulation; neither result rules out a smaller\\nskid buffer with precise acceptance and invalidation semantics.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The pipeline currently freezes the fetch PC whenever dmem or the iterative\\ndivider blocks decode, even when imem is ready. Those otherwise useful fetch\\ncycles cannot hide a later random imem stall. The registered fetch queue\\nexperiment lost cycles by requiring refill after redirects, while the prior\\nfall-through attempt failed cosimulation; neither result rules out a smaller\\nskid buffer with precise acceptance and invalidation semantics.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decouple the fetch address from the decode consumer with a single-entry\\nfall-through skid buffer. When the entry is empty, the current ready imem\\nresponse feeds ID combinationally with exactly the present front-end latency.\\nIf downstream stalls, capture that accepted instruction and advance the fetch\\naddress once; when downstream resumes, drain the saved instruction while\\noptionally accepting its sequential successor in the same cycle. A redirect\\nhas absolute priority, invalidates the saved entry, installs the resolved PC,\\nand exposes the target response through the empty fall-through path on the\\nfollowing cycle, so taken-control-flow recovery gains no refill bubble. This\\nshould overlap imem service with dmem/divider stalls and reduce CoreMark cycles\\nwhile adding only one instruction/PC register and shallow valid control, with\\nno predictor lookup or target arithmetic on the fetch-PC recurrence.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the coupled PC/IF handoff with a one-entry fall-through skid buffer, advancing the fetch address only on an accepted imem response and giving redirects priority to discard buffered wrong-path state without a target refill bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate front-end consumer backpressure from imem response validity so dmem and divider stalls request skid capture, while an imem stall suppresses delivery without corrupting buffered state or redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the skid buffer's accepted-instruction and downstream-ready controls into the existing nret=1 pipeline without changing top-level IO, retirement ordering, load bypassing, or long-operation behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized checks for capture during dmem/divider stalls, empty fall-through delivery, simultaneous drain/refill, prolonged imem stalls, redirects with empty and full buffers, exact RVFI PCs/order, and absence of duplicate or dropped instructions.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the coupled PC/IF handoff with a one-entry fall-through skid buffer, advancing the fetch address only on an accepted imem response and giving redirects priority to discard buffered wrong-path state without a target refill bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate front-end consumer backpressure from imem response validity so dmem and divider stalls request skid capture, while an imem stall suppresses delivery without corrupting buffered state or redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the skid buffer's accepted-instruction and downstream-ready controls into the existing nret=1 pipeline without changing top-level IO, retirement ordering, load bypassing, or long-operation behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized checks for capture during dmem/divider stalls, empty fall-through delivery, simultaneous drain/refill, prolonged imem stalls, redirects with empty and full buffers, exact RVFI PCs/order, and absence of duplicate or dropped instructions.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 368.03, \"delta_pct\": -10.86, \"vs_baseline\": 30.13, \"fmax_mhz\": 154.89, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4208643, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5730, \"ff\": 2266, \"seeds\": [158.81, 154.56, 154.89], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:54:54.057424Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Speculative low-tag last-loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Taken CoreMark loop backedges still incur two redirect bubbles, but all prior\\npredictors put a full tag comparison, instruction decode, target arithmetic,\\nor indexed table lookup on the fetch-PC recurrence and lost more Fmax than\\ntheir cycle savings recovered. The latest one-entry loop predictor isolated\\nthe remaining dominant cost: an exact 32-bit PC comparison before selecting\\nthe registered target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Taken CoreMark loop backedges still incur two redirect bubbles, but all prior\\npredictors put a full tag comparison, instruction decode, target arithmetic,\\nor indexed table lookup on the fetch-PC recurrence and lost more Fmax than\\ntheir cycle savings recovered. The latest one-entry loop predictor isolated\\nthe remaining dominant cost: an exact 32-bit PC comparison before selecting\\nthe registered target.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retain one registered backward-branch PC and resolved target, but use only six\\nlow PC bits as the speculative fetch trigger. This makes the fetch recurrence\\na small constant-width equality test plus the target mux, with no full tag,\\nopcode decode, target adder, counter, or indexed array. Carry a prediction bit\\nand a small entry generation value with the instruction; in EX, off the fetch\\nrecurrence, verify the complete branch PC, generation, taken outcome, and\\nresolved target. Any low-bit alias, loop exit, stale entry, or target mismatch\\nredirects to the architectural next PC, while a verified repeated backedge\\nsuppresses the normal redirect. Training only accepted taken backward\\nconditional branches should capture hot loops and remove their redirect\\nbubbles while making the timing cost substantially smaller than the failed\\nexact-PC one-entry design.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/loop_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry registered backward-loop target with a six-bit speculative trigger, full PC tag, and generation state; train only from accepted taken backward conditional branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry only prediction-valid and compact generation metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so the full tag check remains outside the fetch recurrence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered loop target on the six-bit trigger while preserving redirect priority and imem stall semantics, without instruction predecode or target arithmetic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the compact prediction metadata under the existing flush and stall rules without changing decode legality or RVFI source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Verify the prediction using the full stored tag, generation, resolved direction, and target; recover every alias, stale prediction, and loop exit while suppressing redirects only for exact correct predictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire speculative lookup, EX-side verification, recovery, and stall-qualified training through the existing nret=1 pipeline without changing top-level IO or retirement order.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, same-low-bits alias, loop-exit, entry-replacement, stale-generation, and randomized bus-stall tests proving wrong-path suppression, exact RVFI PCs, strict order, and forward progress.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/loop_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry registered backward-loop target with a six-bit speculative trigger, full PC tag, and generation state; train only from accepted taken backward conditional branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry only prediction-valid and compact generation metadata through IF/ID and ID/EX so the full tag check remains outside the fetch recurrence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered loop target on the six-bit trigger while preserving redirect priority and imem stall semantics, without instruction predecode or target arithmetic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the compact prediction metadata under the existing flush and stall rules without changing decode legality or RVFI source reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Verify the prediction using the full stored tag, generation, resolved direction, and target; recover every alias, stale prediction, and loop exit while suppressing redirects only for exact correct predictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire speculative lookup, EX-side verification, recovery, and stall-qualified training through the existing nret=1 pipeline without changing top-level IO or retirement order.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, same-low-bits alias, loop-exit, entry-replacement, stale-generation, and randomized bus-stall tests proving wrong-path suppression, exact RVFI PCs, strict order, and forward progress.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 216.84, \"delta_pct\": -47.48, \"vs_baseline\": -23.33, \"fmax_mhz\": 90.83, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4188794, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5918, \"ff\": 2270, \"seeds\": [84.26, 96.37, 90.83], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:57:13.045441Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (regression, -47.48%): Shrinking a one-entry loop predictor's fetch tag to six PC bits still regresses badly because even a small equality test and registered-target mux remain on the fetch-PC recurrence, so comparison width alone cannot rescue direct next-PC prediction without staging or decoupling the target select.\"}\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],